# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# EXP-1: the fog covers the squares the party has not been near, and
# nothing else on the screen.
#
# **A pair leg, and it can be one.** This seam claims no key — it is a
# setting, not a command (`seam_explored.cpp`) — so every keystroke in
# the script below reaches the program in both halves and the two runs
# differ by the flag alone. That is the one thing the journal's legs
# could not have (#234).
#
# **What changed with M5-E5f (#263).** The marking used to be a lift:
# the game's whole window as the game drew it, with the squares the party
# had walked one shade brighter. So the two runs were identical until the
# party took its first step, and the first `allow` below covered the
# whole arrival with `none`. The fog is the other way round — a map
# nobody has walked is a window that is nearly all fog — so the two runs
# now diverge at frame 10,275, which is where the arrival settles, and
# what this leg confines is the fog rather than the trail.
#
# **What the fog is, since this leg is what confines it.** A covered
# square is *half* covered: a one-pixel checkerboard of palette index 8,
# the game's own dark grey, with the program's own pixel on the other
# half (M5-E5f, #263, after the maintainer chose it off five coverings
# composited over a real frame). The rects below did not move for that —
# confinement is about the window, and a checker reaches exactly the
# squares a solid fill reached — but the picture inside them did, which
# is why `wild-trail.rec` was recorded again and this file was not.
#
# **The rects are the fogged squares and not a box round them.** At a
# reveal radius of one the party sees the three-by-three it is standing
# in, so on this walk the window's outer ring is covered and its middle
# three columns are not — and for the frames before the first step the
# row *behind* the party is covered too, because the reveal has not
# reached it yet. Naming the window as one rectangle would have passed
# whatever the seam drew inside it; these four say where the boundary is.
# Measured on a real run: 523 dumped frames, 112 of them differing from
# the seam-off run, every difference inside the four rects below and not
# one pixel outside the window at any frame.
about the fog covers the squares the party has not been near, and nothing else
kind pair
program START.EXE
both code-wheel
# The code-wheel bypass is gated on the wheel itself (#115), and both
# sides of this pair drive past the challenge, so both need it. Pinned
# by digest because a document never enters this tree.
document 0db301aeac4d2ec1e63b409ca6d3c9d39c63381d6712ff2ce7edc0528c6586fd
seam explored
until 260000000
every 25
# A screen may appear in the two runs a dump apart, because a repaint
# lands where it lands. One dump of slack, the same as every other leg.
slack 1

# Slot J: the one shipped save whose party is already standing on a
# wilderness area (`docs/explored-overlay.md` §4). Eight steps north,
# 150 frames apart, which is the cadence every driven walk in this tree
# uses.
key A@7601
key Return@7651
key L@8951
key J@9201
key Up@10600
key Up@10750
key Up@10900
key Up@11050
key Up@11200
key Up@11350
key Up@11500
key Up@11650

# The window is 120 by 120 at (8, 8) and a square is 24 by 24, so these
# are whole squares of it: the row the party is walking towards, the two
# flanking columns beside its corridor, and — until the first step — the
# row behind it.
rect ahead 8,8,127,31
rect west 8,32,31,127
rect east 104,32,127,127
rect behind 32,104,103,127

# The whole run up to the arrival on the wilderness map: not one pixel.
# The mode byte becomes 3 at frame 9,552 and the screen settles at
# 10,275, which is where the fog first appears.
allow 0-10250 none
# Arrived, with one square stood on: the outer ring and the row behind.
allow 10275-10650 ahead west east behind
# And from the first step on, the reveal has caught up with the row
# behind and the corridor is three wide for the rest of the walk.
allow 10675-end ahead west east
